note How an icon reaches the screen as text. An icon is not an image: ui's Icon names a glyph in Unicode's Private Use Area, the codepoint Codicons (assets/codicon.ttf, Microsoft, CC BY 4.0, see assets/codicon-LICENSE.txt) gives it, so an icon is a char inside an ordinary Label and Run and mixes with text in one run: a button, a row, a tab or a format!() string holds it the way it holds a letter, and every widget that takes a Label takes an icon (From<Icon> for Label). Because icons are square and cells are about 0.6 em wide, an icon glyph is two columns: Label::columns, Label::wrap, the canvas's pen and the renderer's pen all advance by Glyph::columns, so layout and drawing agree. The renderer's font holds two faces; a Private Use Area glyph is rasterised from the icon face, sized to fit its two-cell box and one row, centred in it, and cached in the same atlas under the same (size, glyph) key as every letter, so an icon costs one textured quad in the same batch as the text around it. Dumps spell an icon as [name], so scripts stay readable.

